{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310640,"nonce":"g1VcWvcTIErg2anXpogF+p4Vws4=","title":"nue/llama-chat fails ~60% of cold-cache runs with non-deterministic heap corruption during the streaming decode loop. Manifests three ways: ...","message":"Comprehensive session arc consolidated in [project_session_2026_06_compiler_arc.md](project_session_2026_06_compiler_arc.md).\n\n**HEAD: b013900** (committed). Track B (user_function_name_map mirror + cranelift call-site name fallback) was attempted on top of b013900, measured no-change, and **REVERTED**.\n\n**Measurements at b013900, Track B reverted:**\n\n| Scenario | N | Success | Paris | Exit histogram |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Cold-clean canonical | 10 | 9 | 9 | `{\"0\":9, \"133\":1}` |\n| Cold-clean short | 5 | 4 | n/a | `{\"0\":4, \"133\":1}` |\n| Warm-BLADE canonical | 5 | 5 | 5 | `{\"0\":5}` |\n\nLib 709/709, haxe regression 127-128/128 (128th = pre-existing test_exception flake), tiny-transformer green.\n\n**Two remaining bug classes (both OPEN):**\n\n1. **user→user CallDirect renumber gap.** Residuals `BertArch.build → IrFunctionId(280007)`, `LlamaArch.build → IrFunctionId(280007)`, `GGUFTokenizer.build → IrFunctionId(220015)` persist on every cold-clean run. b013900 only addresses stdlib half; user-side requires the symmetric user_function_name_map infrastructure WITH instrumentation to discriminate seed-ordering vs qualified_name-None.\n\n2. **Runtime SIGTRAP 133 at first decode iteration (content-dependent, KVCache vicinity).** The 1/10 residual at canonical paris and 1/5 at short prompts. NOT the trap-stub cascade (Path E + cranelift_backend assert would surface E0600 if it were). Crash is AFTER `[gen] streaming...` prints but BEFORE first token emits, in/near KVCache.append at seq_pos=1. H-kvcache-append-stride refuted (contiguous workaround no-change). Open sub-mechanisms: attention-mask OOB, GQA head-expand stride at decode, RoPE/expandKvHeads NaN/inf trapping bmm. Next: eprintln K/V shape+strides+finite-count before cache.append, dump qByHead/kT/vAllExpanded before bmmThreaded, diff success vs fail across 10 cold short runs.\n\n**Next-iteration plan (concrete):** Reapply Track B's source patches, add `eprintln!` pre-fixup dump of `user_function_name_map` AND every user MIR module's `func.qualified_name`/`func.name`/`func.id`, run llama-chat cold once, search stderr for `280007` and `220015`:\n- Both ids appear as values in map → seed ordering is bug; hoist seed before fixup invocation.\n- Ids in user_fn_table but not user_fn_map → empty-CFG guard too restrictive; drop guard.\n- Neither channel has them → monomorph specialisations with qualified_name=None; extend seed to also write bare names + bare-name fallback in cranelift_backend.\n\nOriginal \"60% streaming heap corruption / worker_pool race\" framing remains CLOSED (sections below kept for archaeology). The actual class was trap-stub cascade from stale CallDirects, not heap corruption.\n\n---\n\n\nTrack B landed the user_function_name_map (symmetric mirror of stdlib_function_name_map) per the previous section's fix path: (a) BTreeMap field on `CompilationUnit`, (b) seed loop after each user mir_module merge (`compilation.rs` ~line 5732), (c) `id_replacements` renumber sweep after stdlib merge (~line 5654), (d) parallel walk over `mir_modules` in `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` consulting the user map first then falling back to stdlib, (e) qualified_name population for every user function in `cranelift_backend.rs::declare_function`, (f) call-site last-resort lookup via `qualified_name_to_func` when `function_map.get(func_id)` misses.\n\n**MEASURE (cold-clean canonical \"What is the capital of France?\", N=10):**\n- verdict: **no-change**\n- exit_histogram: `{\"132\":10}` — 10/10 exit 132 (SIGILL, trap-stub hit at runtime)\n- paris_count: 0/10\n- tiny_transformer_ok: true\n- haxe regression: 127/128\n- bench_tok_per_sec: 0\n- W0020 residuals (all 3 USER → USER, all 3 unchanged from pre-Track-B trace):\n  - `nue.arch.BertArch.build` → `IrFunctionId(280007)`\n  - `nue.arch.LlamaArch.build` → `IrFunctionId(280007)`\n  - `nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.build` → `IrFunctionId(220015)`\n\n\nb013900 (\"refresh stdlib_function_name_map after stdlib merge\") only touches the **stdlib** map. The three residual W0020 callees are USER package symbols (`nue.arch.*`, `nue.loader.*`) — they were never in any map b013900 maintains. The expected scope-extension was Track B's user_function_name_map; with that landed and the cascade still 10/10, the user-name map either (a) isn't being populated for these specific callees, or (b) is populated but the seed runs AFTER the fixup pass walks the callers' modules — so the lookup misses despite the data being present moments later.\n\n\nMost probable: **the user_function_name_map seed is too late.** Track B seeds the map at `mir_modules.push(...)` in `compile_ast_with_shared_state` (~line 5732), which runs per-user-module AFTER stdlib merge + monomorphize + post-merge fixup. The `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` walk that needs the map runs ONCE at the end of the pipeline, but it walks every previously-seeded module — and any caller whose ext_names key resolves to 280007/220015 before the matching callee module finishes its seed will miss. Verify by adding a dump of `user_function_name_map.keys()` right before the fixup pass; if `nue.arch.BertArch.build` callees (whatever name resolves to 280007) are absent, the seed ordering is the bug.\n\nSecondary possibility: **the callee functions have empty CFG at seed time** (forward refs, monomorph stubs) and the seed's `if func.cfg.blocks.is_empty() { continue; }` guard silently drops them — but then a later merge fills in the body under the same id, and Sweep 1 of the stdlib-map approach (drop entries pointing at empty stubs and rewrite to real bodies) never runs against the user map. The user_function_name_map needs the same two-sweep validation pattern that the stdlib map got in iteration 3 (\"Sweep 2 rewrite: build oracle of real bodies, rewrite stub-target entries\").\n\nTertiary: **the IrFunctionIds 280007/220015 aren't qualified-name-routable in the first place** — they might be monomorphization specialisations (e.g. `Array\u003cArchEntry\u003e` for one and a tokenizer-specific helper for the other) whose qualified_name is `None` because monomorph specialisations inherit the bare name. In that case the cranelift_backend.rs call-site fallback's `called_func.qualified_name.as_deref()` returns None → Err path → trap-stub. Confirm by patching the fallback's Err branch to log `called_func.name` and `called_func.qualified_name` side-by-side.\n\n\nEven with no observable behavioural change on llama-chat, the patch:\n1. Closes the user→user fallback gap that **would** have caught the same class of bug at a future user-package symbol drift event (defensive correctness).\n2. Adds the `debug_assert!` message refinement noting Path E + Track B coverage so the next surface refers to the correct closed set.\n3. Establishes the user_function_name_map as a real infrastructure piece that the next iteration's two-sweep validation can extend.\n\nTrack B is sound infrastructure that didn't reach the failure mode it was designed for. The next iteration needs to either (i) move the seed earlier in the pipeline (before any module-level fixup), (ii) add the two-sweep validation the stdlib map already has, or (iii) instrument to confirm whether 280007/220015 actually have qualified_names and thus are routable at all.\n\n\nSame reasoning as the prior section's b013900 decision: Track B closes a real correctness gap (symmetric user-side coverage that didn't exist before). The fact that it doesn't visibly fix llama-chat just means the underlying bug isn't where Track B's coverage extends. Roll back would re-open a future-defensible asymmetry between the stdlib and user paths.\n\n\n1. Add eprintln-instrumented dump of `user_function_name_map` immediately before `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` runs to confirm whether 280007/220015 are present-as-values. Decides between \"seed ordering\" vs \"qualified_name is None\" hypotheses.\n2. If present-but-not-found: move the seed BEFORE `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` in `compile_ast_with_shared_state`'s control flow (post-merge, pre-fixup).\n3. If absent: walk `mir_modules` directly in the fixup pass and lookup by `func.qualified_name.as_deref().unwrap_or(\u0026func.name)` rather than relying on the pre-seeded map. Trades cache for completeness.\n4. If qualified_name is None for 280007/220015: extend the seed to also write under the bare `func.name` key with a marker (e.g. prefix) so the fallback can find them by bare name; OR fix monomorphization to propagate the qualified_name of the parent generic.\n\n---\n\n\nFollowup non-determinism probe compared the \"clean build\" trap-stub set vs the \"incremental build\" trap-stub set, focusing on the two residual IrFunctionIds (280007, 220015) referenced in the W0020 cascade trace below. Result:\n\n- 280007: **absent in BOTH builds** (not in clean trap-stub set, not in incremental trap-stub set)\n- 220015: **absent in BOTH builds** (likewise)\n- novel ids unique to clean build: `[]`\n- novel ids unique to incremental build: `[]`\n\n→ **Decision: `build-states-identical`.** We were wrong about there being a meaningful build-state distinction between the previously-measured 4-8/10 \"incremental working\" runs and the 0/10 \"clean broken\" runs. The two builds produce the same trap-stub universe. The previous rate-difference signal (a 4-8/10 → 0/10 gap interpreted as \"incremental fix surviving, clean rebuild losing it\") was NOT due to b013900 catching residuals in one state but not the other.\n\nThe actual source of the 4-8/10 vs 0/10 difference must be EXTERNAL to b013900's scope. Most likely candidates:\n\n1. **Cache state on disk** — `.rayzor/` directories at the project root vs `compiler/` root surviving across \"clean\" measurements. Per `bugs_rayzor_cache_scrub.md`: per-example BLADE clears miss them; full scrub is `find . -name .rayzor -type d -exec rm -rf {} +` + `cargo clean`. Almost certainly the dominant noise source.\n2. **Environment differences between measurement sessions** — `RAYZOR_TIER=llvm` vs Cranelift default, `RUST_LOG=debug` affecting timing, terminal width changing tracing output, prior run leaving warm OS file cache.\n3. **Measurement noise** — N=10 binomial confidence for the 4/10 baseline is wide: 95% CI roughly [12%, 74%]. A subsequent 0/10 from the SAME population is within the lower tail (≈6% chance for a fixed p=40%). Not extreme; could be honest variance.\n\n\n**Do NOT roll back b013900.** The fix's intended target (stdlib_function_name_map stale-stub-id rewriting) is a real correctness gap closed by the commit; both the \"renumber name-map values after stdlib merge\" change and the Path E `debug_assert!` on CallDirect targets are sound in isolation. The 280007/220015 residuals shown in the trace-diff trap-stub set are USER-module CallDirects (`LlamaArch.build`, `BertArch.build`, `GGUFTokenizer.build`) — these were never in b013900's scope. b013900 attacks the stdlib renumber gap; the user→user CallDirect renumber gap remains unfixed (see `fix-path` section in `bugs_trap_stub_cascade.md`).\n\n\nTwo independent tracks:\n\n**Track A — Reproduce the rate signal cleanly.** Before any further hypothesis work:\n1. Full scrub: `find . -name .rayzor -type d -exec rm -rf {} +` + `cargo clean`.\n2. Single fixed env: `RAYZOR_TIER=cranelift`, no `RUST_LOG`, no `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS`.\n3. Build once, run llama-chat 30 times (N=30 not 10) with `--prompt \"What is the capital of France?\"`.\n4. Compute exit_code histogram + paris_match_count. Repeat the full scrub + 30-run cycle 3 times back-to-back.\n5. If all three cycles show a CONSISTENT rate (e.g. all three ≈ 4/30, or all three ≈ 25/30), we have a real baseline. If they swing wildly (4/30, 20/30, 0/30), the measurement is non-stationary and we need to control for OS-level state (page cache, ASLR, scheduler) before any compiler claim is meaningful.\n\n**Track B — Close the user→user CallDirect gap.** Independent of the rate signal:\n1. Per the trace-diff section's fix path: build `user_function_name_map` (symmetric to `stdlib_function_name_map`) keyed by qualified name → final renumbered id, populated as user modules merge.\n2. Extend `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` in `compiler/src/compilation.rs` to consult this map on user→user CallDirect targets.\n3. In `cranelift_backend.rs::compile_module` (≈line 929), when `compile_function` fails with \"Called function IrFunctionId(N) not found in module\", attempt a last-resort lookup against the merged-modules' `func_id_to_qualified_name + bare_name_to_id_set` index before installing the trap-stub.\n4. Verify by re-running `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` against the failing build — 280007 and 220015 should no longer appear in the W0020 trap-stub list.\n\n\nIt does NOT classify whether the 60%-fail surface is (i) the W0020 cascade alone, (ii) the W0020 cascade plus a value-domain crash, or (iii) something else entirely. Track A + Track B are prerequisites; only after the rate signal stabilises AND the user→user gap closes can we tell whether any value-domain crash mechanism (RoPE NaN, broadcast OOB, mask OOB from the earlier trichotomy) is also live.\n\n---\n\n\nDiscriminator was supposed to compare success vs fail `[gqa-pre-bmm]` / `[gqa-pre-append]` instrumentation traces to pick between hyp1 (RoPE NaN), hyp2 (broadcast OOB), hyp3 (mask OOB).\n\n**The data refuses the trichotomy.** Success=0/10, Fail=10/10. The fail-tail last-traces show ONLY compile-time noise:\n- `Warning: [W0020] cannot compile nue.arch.BertArch.build; reason: Called function IrFunctionId(280007) not found in module`\n- `Warning: [W0020] cannot compile nue.arch.LlamaArch.build; reason: IrFunctionId(280007) not found in module`\n- `Warning: [W0020] cannot compile nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.build; reason: IrFunctionId(220015) not found in module`\n- `[tier] manifest requested auto-upgrade to LLVM, but this build was compiled without llvm-backend; continuing on Cranelift`\n\nNEITHER `[gqa-pre-bmm]` NOR `[gqa-pre-append]` ever fires. NEITHER does `[gen] streaming...`, `[load] done`, `[lwt]` etc. Crash is BEFORE the load even completes — at first invocation of one of the W0020 trap-stubbed user functions.\n\n**Verdict: h-other.** This is the **W0020 trap-stub cascade** class (same family as iterations 1-5 in the SUPERSEDED FRAMING section), not a value-domain RoPE/broadcast/mask bug. The fact that there are zero success runs is the giveaway: a value-domain bug (RoPE NaN, etc.) would manifest in some intermittent shape, not 10/10 fail with the same compile-time warnings.\n\nThe `IrFunctionId(280007)` referenced from `nue.arch.BertArch.build` AND `nue.arch.LlamaArch.build` is the same callee from two different caller files — strongly suggests a single missing user→user CallDirect target (probably an Array helper or interface dispatch trampoline introduced around the `[bugs_iface_to_iface_cast_pass_through]` triple-commit a8cef3c/c7a170d/b9f407d, which is the same era as commit `__vtable_init__` restriction landing).\n\n`IrFunctionId(220015)` from `GGUFTokenizer.build` is a separate cluster — likely a different bare-name builtin under the user-module renumber path.\n\n\nRoot-cause cluster: the `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` pass (compilation.rs:3572) does NOT close the user→user CallDirect renumber gap for these three caller modules. The previous iteration's Fix B (sweeps over `stdlib_function_name_map`) only repairs stdlib targets; the residuals 280007/220015 live in the USER module table.\n\n1. **compiler/src/compilation.rs**: build a `user_function_name_map` (symmetric to `stdlib_function_name_map`) keyed by qualified name → final renumbered id, populated as user modules merge. Extend `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` to consult this map on user→user CallDirect targets, mirroring the stdlib branch.\n2. **compiler/src/codegen/cranelift_backend.rs:929 (compile_module loop)**: when `compile_function` fails with \"Called function IrFunctionId(N) not found in module\", before installing the trap-stub, attempt a last-resort lookup against the merged-modules' `func_id_to_qualified_name + bare_name_to_id_set` index. Only trap-stub if that fallback ALSO misses.\n3. Confirm with `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` on a fail run — surface the qualified name the stale ids 280007 and 220015 were trying to reach. That tells us which user→user edge (likely a vtable trampoline, given the era) needs the renumber.\n\nThe previous trace-driven hyp1/hyp2/hyp3 instrumentation is moot until the trap-stub cascade closes — no instrumented decode code will ever run.\n\n---\n\n\nProbe: insert `.contiguous()` calls on K and V immediately before `cache.append(k, v)` at `nue/nue/transformer/GQAttention.hx:129` to force materialised contiguous buffers and bypass any stride-walking ambiguity inside `rayzor_tensor_append_along0`.\n\n**MEASURE**: SIGTRAP 7/10 (vs ~6-8/10 baseline), Paris canonical 3/5, tok/s 15.173, verdict=**no-change**.\n\n→ **H-kvcache-append-stride REFUTED.** Forcing contiguous source tensors did not move the failure rate at all. The trap is NOT in append's memcpy-vs-stride-walk logic. The previous H-kvcache-edge \"confirmed\" verdict pointed at the right LOCATION (first decode iter, KVCache vicinity) but the WRONG MECHANISM (append stride handling). Two surviving sub-mechanisms from H-kvcache-edge:\n1. Attention mask construction at seq_pos=1 producing OOB index for specific KV depths.\n2. GQA head-expansion stride correct for prefill `[T, hidden]` but wrong for decode `[1, hidden]` at certain promptLen values.\n3. NEW: Value-domain NaN/inf from RoPE pair-split or expandKvHeads emitting denormals — `bmmThreaded(kT, 0)` could trap on FP signal.\n\nThe append-side fix (stride-aware `rayzor_tensor_append_along0` in `runtime/src/tensor.rs`) is no longer warranted — it would touch correct code with a misdiagnosed cause.\n\n\nInstrument BEFORE the append, not at the runtime entry:\n1. eprintln K/V shape, strides, finite-count, |max|, |mean| inside `GQAttention.forward` at line 128 (just before `cache.append(k, v)`).\n2. Also dump `qByHead`/`kT`/`vAllExpanded` shape+strides at lines 138-143 BEFORE `bmmThreaded`.\n3. Replay 10 cold short-prompt runs. Compare success vs fail across (a) any NaN/inf in K/V/Q/scoresRaw, (b) any stride mismatch on the bmm inputs at decode (seq_pos=1) that differs from prefill.\n\nThe crash is upstream of append. Likely candidates in priority order: (i) RoPE half-split producing NaN at a specific positionOffset, (ii) expandKvHeads broadcast view emitting an invalid stride at decode step 1, (iii) bmm reading past the seq_pos=1 slice into uninitialised KV cache rows beyond `currentLen`.\n\n---\n\n\nThree-probe discriminator run on the SIGTRAP 133 surface from the section below:\n\n**Probe 1 — `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` on short prompt (N=10).**\n- SIGTRAP rate: 6/10\n- W0020 observed: **false**\n- New trap-stub entries beyond baseline: **none** (4 entries flagged PRE-EXISTING benign — `ArrayKeyValueIterator.new`, `ArrayIterator.new`, `TreeNode.new`, `BalancedTree.new`; one __reflect_ctor_wrap cascade matches benign pattern with varying wrap suffix)\n- Sample fail-tail terminates at `[gen] streaming...` with empty stderr — **no panic, no W0020, no Rust panic surface**\n\n→ **H-trap-stub-leak REFUTED** (no new trap-stub entries; W0020=false). The Path E + cranelift_backend assert chain is doing its job; no silent dispatch is being emitted.\n→ **H-runtime-panic REFUTED** (stderr would carry `panicked at` if abort() came from Rust panic; it doesn't).\n\n**Probe 2 — Prompt-length: \"describe the colour of the sky please\" (~8 tokens).**\n- SIGTRAP rate: 8/10 (vs 6/10 on `test` short ~3 tokens, vs 2/10 on `What is the capital of France?` 9 tokens)\n- Verdict: **content-matters**, not raw-length-matters\n\n→ The 8-token sky-prompt failing 80% while the 9-token Paris-prompt fails only 20% rules out a pure \"prompt_len \u003c N → trap\" gate. Content (specific token-id ranges / KV-cache shape at first decode step) drives the fail rate.\n\n**Probe 3 — Tier promotion disabled (`enable_tier_promotion=false` + `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry=false` in [tier]).**\n- SIGTRAP rate with tier off: **6/10** (statistically identical to 6/10 baseline)\n- Verdict: **tier-not-the-cause**\n\n→ **H4 (tier-2 swap mid-decode) REFUTED.** Tier promotion is not what's flipping the dispatch table mid-flight; the bug exists at the Cranelift Baseline tier alone.\n\n\nPer the hypothesis rubric supplied with the probe set:\n- H-kvcache-edge: \"prompt length matters BUT tier disable doesn't fix → likely H\"\n\nBoth clauses hold (prompt content matters per Probe 2; tier disable doesn't fix per Probe 3). The crash position (AFTER `[gen] streaming...` prints, BEFORE any token is emitted) is precisely the first decode iteration at seq_pos=1 — the same KVCache.append boundary that the original SIGSEGV surface targeted (`KVCache.append failed (krc=-1, vrc=-1)` from the doc's iteration-1 symptom matrix). The signal-class shifted (SIGSEGV→SIGTRAP) but the location and content-sensitivity match the seq_pos=1 first-decode edge.\n\nH2 (StringMap UAF on concat keys) is also consistent with \"no fn-ptr drift + content matters\" — different prompts hit different metadata key strings — but the crash location (after `[gen] streaming...`, i.e. AFTER `metadataFromReader` ran successfully through `[lwt] 8 → [load] done`) makes it unlikely; StringMap UAF would surface during the GGUF metadata-load phase, not during streaming decode.\n\n\nThe first decode call with seq_pos=1 (KV cache holding only the prompt tokens) hits a content-dependent code path that traps roughly 60–80% of the time. Likely sub-mechanisms:\n1. KVCache.append at seq_pos=prompt_len with content-specific shape mismatch (e.g. a stride that's only valid for some prompt lengths).\n2. Attention mask construction at seq_pos=1 producing an out-of-bounds index for specific KV depths.\n3. GQA head-expansion stride that's correct for prefill `[promptLen, hidden]` but wrong for decode `[1, hidden]` when promptLen lands in certain ranges.\n\n\n1. Inspect `nue/nue/transformer/attention/GQAttention.hx` (and KVCache.append) for any code path that branches on seq_pos==1 or seq_pos==prompt_len. Look specifically for stride/shape arithmetic that mixes prefill-shape `[B, T, H]` and decode-shape `[B, 1, H]` assumptions.\n2. Add a deterministic eprintln guard in `KVCache.append`: log `(seq_pos, keys.shape, values.shape, krc, vrc)` on every call. Replay 10 cold short-prompt runs and diff what changes between the 4 success and 6 fail cases at the first decode iteration.\n3. Cross-reference Phase (c) GQAttention fix in `project_llama_chat_coherence_phase_c.md` — that fix made `qByHead.bmm(kT)` stride-aware via permute. Audit whether KVCache.append is similarly stride-aware when receiving a permuted/transposed-view tensor as input.\n\n\n`/run` 10 cold short-prompt invocations with `eprintln` instrumentation around KVCache.append entry. Capture (seq_pos, krc, vrc, dest_shape, src_shape) on both success and fail paths. If fail-path consistently shows a specific (krc, vrc) signature at seq_pos==prompt_len, the fix is in append's bounds/stride handling. If the eprintln itself never fires on fail (i.e. the trap happens BEFORE KVCache.append is reached), the bug moves up one frame to the attention forward call, narrowing to GQAttention's bmm/transpose chain.\n\n---\n\n\n20-run cold-cache exit-code histogram resolves the prior empty-capture ambiguity:\n\n- **short prompt (\"test\", N=10):** exit_histogram `{\"0\":4, \"133\":6}` — 4/10 clean, 6/10 SIGTRAP. Every fail shows last_stdout=`[dbg.prompt-id] 10 271\\n[gen] streaming...` and empty stderr — i.e. crash AFTER prefill prints but DURING the first decode iter. Same \"after `[gen] streaming...`\" timing as the original heap-corruption surface, but signal class is now 133 (SIGTRAP / Trace-BPT) not 139 (SIGSEGV).\n- **canonical match prompt (\"What is the capital of France?\", N=10):** exit_histogram `{\"0\":8, \"133\":2}`, paris_count=8/10. First samples are clean `The capital of France is Paris.`.\n\n**Interpretation: bug-shifted-shape.** The 4/10 baseline this doc opened with was SIGSEGV exit 139 + heap corruption (`addInto src=0x0`, `KVCache.append -1 -1`, libmalloc EXC_BREAKPOINT). The current 4/10-short / 8/10-canonical baseline is SIGTRAP exit 133 — classic Cranelift W0020 trap-stub cascade or BLADE-cache stale fn-ptr signature per `bugs_llama_chat_cached_run_crash.md`. The signal class change rules out the original heap-corruption hypotheses (H1 anon-vtable, H2 StringMap-UAF, H5 null-deref) and instead implicates the dispatch/trap-stub class.\n\n**Prior workflow's \"0/10 SIGSEGV\" was correct, not a classifier bug** — there really are zero SIGSEGV observations because the bug surface has SHIFTED to SIGTRAP. The previous SIGSEGV-handler harness was reading the truth; it just couldn't say \"no SIGSEGV because no SIGSEGV exists\" vs \"no SIGSEGV because handler dropped them\". This 20-run exit-code accounting disambiguates: zero exit-139, six+two exit-133. Real shape change.\n\n\nShort \"test\" (single token, no real conversation) fails 60%; long \"What is the capital of France?\" fails 20%. Both follow the same JIT/MIR path but exercise different token-id ranges and different attention shapes. Two ranked hypotheses:\n\n1. **Prompt-length-dependent dispatch path**. The short prompt has prompt_len ~= 3; the canonical prompt has prompt_len ~= 9-10. Decode step 1 in the short case operates on a near-empty KV cache; in the canonical case the KV cache is meaningfully warm before any decode call. If a specific tier-promotion / BLADE-cached fn ptr is invoked only when KV depth \u003c threshold, the trap-stub hits the short prompt preferentially.\n2. **Token-id-range path divergence**. Different prompts tokenize to different ids, hit different vocab-shard branches in lm_head (Q6_K block index), and potentially fall on stale trap-stubbed dispatch entries for one branch but not another. The 8/10 vs 4/10 split could be statistical from which Q6_K block the first decoded token lands in.\n\n\nNOT \"declare resolved\" — short prompt is still 60% fail. NOT \"rebuild handler\" — the previous handler was correct, the data was real. The right move is:\n\n1. **Run lldb attach on a failing short-prompt invocation** with `process handle SIGTRAP --pass false --stop true`, capture the PC at the trap. The PC distinguishes (a) Cranelift trap-stub (JIT region 0x100000000-0x200000000, instruction `udf` / `ud2`) from (b) BLADE-cache stale fn-ptr (lands in libsystem region or zeroed page) from (c) a real Rust panic surfacing as SIGTRAP via abort().\n2. **Rerun 10 cold short-prompts with `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1`** to surface any silent trap-stub the Cranelift backend emitted at JIT time. Per `bugs_trap_stub_cascade.md` this is the diagnostic for the W0020 cascade class.\n3. **Test the prompt-length hypothesis cleanly**: run 10 cold runs with prompt=\"test test test test test test test test test\" (9 tokens, matches canonical length). If fail rate drops to ~20%, prompt-length confirmed and the bug is in the short-prompt decode dispatch. If fail rate stays at ~60%, the prompt content matters, not length, and we're closer to a token-id-dependent dispatch path.\n\n\nThe original \"60% streaming heap corruption SIGSEGV\" framing is **CLOSED** (zero SIGSEGV across 20 runs). Replaced by a new \"prompt-dependent SIGTRAP 133 after `[gen] streaming...`\" framing — same crash location, different signal class, different probable cause (trap-stub cascade or BLADE replay, not heap corruption). 8/10 canonical paris-match is a meaningful improvement over the 4/10 paris baseline at the top of this doc and over the historical 0/10 cold-baseline reported by the 5-iteration compiler-side fix stack.\n\n---\n\n\nCapture harness produced **zero SIGSEGV observations across 10 runs** (`PC addresses: []`, `Fault addresses: []`, `Sample backtraces: []`, `consistent_pc: false`). This is itself a strong signal but NOT a classification of any of H1-H5.\n\nReading the capture literally:\n- Either all 10 runs completed without SIGSEGV (a real change in pass rate — possibly tied to the post-901ccb7 anon-field-type-inference fix or the b9f407d/c7a170d/a8cef3c iface-vtable replay chain landing on main since this bug doc was last updated), OR\n- All 10 runs failed in a non-SIGSEGV mode (SIGTRAP exit 133 / SIGABRT 134 / exit 101 from Rust panic / exit 137 from OOM) which the harness's \"signal 139 only\" classifier silently drops, OR\n- The harness ran a build whose runtime panic-handler intercepts the SIGSEGV via `signal()` and exits cleanly with a non-139 code before the kernel posts SIGSEGV — making the harness see \"no segfault\" even though the program died.\n\n**Verdict per schema = `unknown`.** The 5-hypothesis ranking from prior diagnose cannot be confirmed or refuted by an empty capture. PC region = `other` (no PC was ever observed). Fault address pattern = none observed; cannot infer from `[]`.\n\n\nThe prior diagnose surfaced 5 hypotheses (anon-vtable, StringMap-UAF, exposed-uninit, tier2-swap, or-pattern-null) all of which would manifest as SIGSEGV exit 139 on macOS. A 0/10 SIGSEGV rate looks like a fix landed BUT cold-cache llama-chat history (this doc, iterations 3-5) shows the same workload alternating between 0/10 SIGSEGV (clean), 0/10 success + 10/10 SIGTRAP (W0020 trap-stub cascade), and 4/10 success + 6/10 SIGSEGV depending on which compiler/runtime patches were live. An empty PC list is consistent with at least 4 different states; the harness's exit-code histogram is needed to discriminate.\n\n\n1. **Re-run with broader signal capture** — patch