workaround broke things; ignore.** The Haxe-side rewrite did NOT discriminate hypothesis 1 from hypothesis 2 — instead it regressed cold-cache success from the prior 4/10 baseline down to 0/10, with crash signature unchanged (still SIGSEGV inside `metadataFromReader` between `[lwt] 3` and `[lwt] 4`). The discriminator is INVALID as a hypothesis 1 confirm/deny signal because the workaround itself altered the surface.\n\nWhat this leaves unproven:\n- Whether the crash is the anon-literal UAF (hypothesis 1) — still possible but not confirmed by this run.\n- Whether the crash is StringMap UAF on transient concat keys (hypothesis 2) — still untested.\n- Whether the rewrite path itself perturbed BLADE cache state / scope-frees in a way that produced a NEW failure shape sharing the same crash location.\n\n**Recommended next workflow:** rerun the discriminator with a DIFFERENT shape — hoist the `arch + \".\"` concat results into named local strings BEFORE every readIntOr/readFloatOr call (the StringMap-key hypothesis 2 surface) while leaving the anon literal intact. If that improves cold success, hypothesis 2 is the surface; if not, both fall and the bug is upstream (likely the user-module IrFunctionId(12) cluster from iteration 3 — `nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.build` / `GeluFFN.parameters` / `SwiGLU.forward` — i.e. the user→user CallDirect renumber gap that `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` still doesn't catch).\n\nThe REAL fix path (regardless of which surface confirms):\n1. **If hypothesis 2 confirms**: extend `InsertFreePass::mark_escaping` per `bugs_mark_escaping_incomplete.md` to recursively walk composite expression arguments to extern calls, so transient `String` concat results passed into `haxe_stringmap_get` get marked escaping for the duration of the call (currently only bare-Variable args are walked — composite exprs silently no-op'd before the 901ccb7 fix; need to verify the call-arg case is covered).\n2. **If hypothesis 1 confirms (next probe needed)**: fix is in compiler — anon vtable / shape descriptor reconstruction during cross-module IMPORT merge. Likely site is `compilation.rs`'s IMPORT-merge path that does NOT carry `next_anon_shape_id` or the shape-id assignment table across modules, so the same shape_id can map to different field layouts in different modules; `rayzor_anon_set_field_by_index` then writes through an index that's correct for the CALLER's shape table but wrong for the CALLEE's. Secondary site is `hir_to_mir.rs:23150 / 23532 / 23681 / 33521` where `self.next_anon_shape_id += 1` runs per-module without cross-module dedup.\n3. **If neither hypothesis confirms**: pivot to the iteration-3 residual — the user→user `IrFunctionId(12)` cluster is a separate bug; build a `user_function_name_map` symmetric to `stdlib_function_name_map` and rerun `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` against it.\n\n---\n\n\nCompiler-side stale-CallDirect cascade (stdlib_function_name_map renumber + qualified_name on stubs + Path E strict assert + per-CallDirect cranelift_backend assert) is FIXED end-to-end. Verified with the three uncommitted edits applied to working tree (compilation.rs:5533-5555, hir_to_mir.rs:6722-6748, cranelift_backend.rs:2796-2802).\n\nNew cold-cache repro: **4/10 success, 6/10 SIGSEGV (exit 139)**. Crash signature shifted:\n\n- Last stdout marker before crash is now `[lwt] 3` — i.e. crash happens INSIDE `metadataFromReader(reader)` between the `var reader = new GGUFReader(bytes)` line and the next trace `[lwt] 4`. Streaming decode is no longer reached on the 6/10 fail path; this is an EARLIER crash than the original streaming heap-corruption surface.\n- Not SIGTRAP (133) — pure SIGSEGV (139). No W0020 trap-stub warning emitted (Path E + cranelift assert would print it if a CallDirect were unresolved). Bash reports `Segmentation fault: 11`. No Rust panic, no EXC_BREAKPOINT in libsystem_malloc — distinguishes this from the prior libmalloc-freelist surface.\n- 8 IMPORT warnings on every run (Int32 clamp, EnumValueMap root, BytesBuffer low/high/floatToI32/doubleToI64, FPHelper POSITIVE_INFINITY/NEGATIVE_INFINITY/NaN, haxe.io.Input/Output, Int64Helper code, haxe.Int64 high). E0382 use-of-moved-value warnings on `info` and `prompt`. `[tier] manifest requested auto-upgrade to LLVM, but this build was compiled without the llvm-backend feature; continuing on Cranelift`.\n\n\n1. **Anon-object literal field-set still racing with InsertFreePass on transient String keys** (high). `metadataFromReader` builds a 12-field anon (`{ architecture, hiddenSize, … }`) at line 134-147; each field assignment is `rayzor_anon_set_field_by_index`. The `arch + \".\"` prefix produces a fresh transient String for every readIntOr/readFloatOr lookup. Under `haxe_string_free` heap-header reclaim (67bfa91), InsertFreePass may schedule the prefix string free between two sequential setter calls while it is still referenced by a captured anon field — UAF deref. Repro fingerprint: deterministic-same-spot crash inside the same fn match.\n2. **GGUFReader.findMeta StringMap.get UAF on `Bytes.subWithBase`-derived key strings** (high). Every metaInt/metaFloat/metaString lookup allocates a fresh `key` String at the caller and calls `StringMap.get(key)` (haxe_stringmap_get extern). If haxe_string_free's new Box::from_raw header reclaim freed the map's stored key during a prior insertion-rehash, the get's strcmp dereferences freed memory.\n3. **OR-pattern enum-tag load against a non-Enum boxed value** (medium). `metaInt`'s `case U8(x)|I8(x)|…|I64(x): x` arm performs a tag load at GEP offset 0 against `v`. If `findMeta` returned a sentinel/placeholder (because StringMap.get's miss returned raw 0 from the StringMap_null_get bug rather than null), the OR-pattern reads tag from offset 0 of the null/zero pointer → SIGSEGV.\n4. **Cranelift tier-2 promotion fires mid-execution of metadataFromReader and swaps the dispatch pointer for a callee already in flight** (medium). `metadataFromReader` is invoked exactly once on the hot path and runs ~12 readIntOr/readFloatOr/readBoolOr lookups; promotion threshold could fire on the 5th lookup, retiring the old fn ptr while the call ABI still holds it. Probability is roughly equal to (call_count \u003e tier_promotion_threshold) — explains 60/40 rather than 100/0 split.\n5. **Something the compiler fix EXPOSED — `rayzor_anon_set_field_by_index` now correctly dispatches but writes through a stale anon header from the IMPORT-warned modules** (medium-low). Pre-fix the call trap-stubbed and we never reached the runtime write; post-fix the dispatch lands and writes to genuine garbage memory because the anon's vtable came from an IMPORT module that didn't get its types fully reconstructed (the 8 IMPORT warnings hint at this).\n6. **GenerationLoop dispatch + token streaming heap freelist corruption** (low, original framing) — superseded for this repro because crash is BEFORE `[gen] streaming...` prints. Kept only as carry-forward for the warm-cache 6/10 SIGTRAP variant where streaming IS reached.\n\nRecommended discriminator: `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` + lldb attach + `process handle SIGSEGV --pass false --stop true` then `bt` at the trap. PC location distinguishes (1)+(2) [in runtime/haxe_sys.rs strcmp/string_free] from (3) [in JITed metaInt] from (5) [in rayzor_anon_set_field_by_index writing through bad receiver].\n\n---\n\n\n**Verdict (2026-06-03, iteration 5):** verdict=partially-fixed, llama=4/10, match_paris=4/10, first_paris_sample=`The capital of France is Paris.`, bench=2.402 tok/s, tiny=PASS, haxe=127/128, residual_e0600=[] (Path E is silent — the E0600 surface is closed).\n\nThe streaming-decode \"heap corruption\" framing was a downstream symptom of stale `CallDirect` IrFunctionIds resolving to empty forward-ref stubs, then JIT silently emitting W0020 trap-stubs whose 4-byte `udf` corrupted whatever allocation got handed the trapped frame. After 5 compiler iterations the entire stale-cross-module-CallDirect class is closed by construction; cold-cache llama-chat now completes generation 4/10 with the canonical \"Paris\" prompt matching, at 2.402 tok/s (Cranelift). The remaining 6/10 failure shape is no longer this bug class.\n\n\n`stdlib_function_name_map` is seeded during user-package compile with **pre-merge** local stub IrFunctionIds taken straight from each user module's `mir_module.functions`. After the stdlib merge into the user package, `id_replacements` renumbers those local stub ids to their merged-canonical equivalents — but the name map's VALUES are never refreshed by that renumber. The secondary `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` pass then uses those stale stub ids as a \"ground truth\" oracle and **overwrites correct cross-module `CallDirect`s** with stale local stub ids (e.g. `fn470007 → fn12`). The overwrite produces an unresolvable target, Path E raises `E0600`, and absent Path E the JIT silently trap-stubs the caller and cascades.\n\nThis also explains the prior \"60% streaming heap corruption / worker_pool race\" framing: the 40% warm-cache successes happened to land on BLADE-replayed name-map entries that were still live by accident; the 60% failures hit either the trap-stub cascade (silent SIGILL during decode) or a runtime crash on whatever allocation got the trapped frame. **worker_pool is fully exonerated.** The Step-2 \"9/10 sequential\" result is also explained: forcing `f(0, rows)` skipped the matmul code path whose `CallDirect` was trap-stubbed.\n\n\n1. **Extend `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` walk to `user_mir_modules`** with qualified-name re-resolution, mirroring `fixup_stale_constructor_ids`. Closes stale stdlib CallDirect targets.\n2. **Path E — strict assert post-fixup**: any still-unresolved cross-module `CallDirect` becomes a loud `E0600 CallDirect target IrFunctionId(N) in \u003cfn\u003e is unresolvable` compile error rather than a silent W0020 trap-stub.\n3. **`qualified_name` on `register_stdlib_mir_forward_ref` stubs** (`hir_to_mir.rs:6725`) — stubs now join both `func_id_to_qualified_name` AND `bare_name_to_id_set` so the per-CallDirect tertiary fallback can fire for stub ids. Plus two-sweep validation pass over `stdlib_function_name_map` (sweep 1 retain-live, sweep 2 rewrite stub-target → real-body oracle from `cached_stdlib_mir` \u003e `import_mir_modules` \u003e `mir_modules`).\n4. **(this iteration) Refresh `stdlib_function_name_map` via `id_replacements` after Main.hx stdlib merge** — apply the same renumber that runs against `mir_module.functions` to the name map's VALUES, so the secondary fixup pass uses post-merge canonical ids as its oracle. Plus Sweep 1 hardening: do **not** count `cached_stdlib_mir` ids as \"live\" when deciding whether to drop a map entry (cached entries can shadow user-merged bodies that haven't been renumbered yet).\n\n\n- verdict: **partially-fixed**\n- tiny_transformer: true\n- haxe regression: 127/128\n- llama cold: **4/10** (up from 0/10 across iterations 2-4)\n- match_paris_count: **4/10**\n- first_paris_sample: `The capital of France is Paris.`\n- residual_e0600: `[]` (Path E silent — closed)\n- bench_tok_per_sec: **2.402** (Cranelift, decode-only)\n\n\nThe original ~60% streaming heap corruption framing pointed at `worker_pool::global().parallel_rows`. That hypothesis is fully retired: the actual race was the stale name-map overwrite described above, surfacing as different downstream allocations depending on which trap-stubbed function got picked up. The Mutex+Condvar replacement, eager `force_init_global`, and `RAYZOR_WORKER_POOL_SEQUENTIAL=1` patches are abandoned (kept in the INVESTIGATION arc below for archaeology only) and should not be relanded.\n\n---\n\n\nEverything below this line is the historical \"60% streaming heap corruption / worker_pool race\" hypothesis arc that the name-map refresh fix supersedes. The actual mechanism is the pre-merge stub-id seeding described in \"Final root cause\" above; the runtime-side investigation is closed.\n\n\nEarlier-iteration sketch: `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` (in `compiler/src/compilation.rs`) did not walk cross-module CallDirect targets pointing into tree-shaken stdlib modules (`haxe.ds.*`, `haxe.Exception`, `StringBuf`-adjacent). On warm/cold cache restore those CallDirect IrFunctionIds resolved to empty stubs and the JIT silently emitted W0020 trap-stubs; control transferred to a 4-byte `udf` at runtime, and the corruption manifested as null-tensor src in `addInto`, `KVCache.append` -1/-1, and silent libmalloc EXC_BREAKPOINT — depending on which downstream allocation got handed the trapped function's frame.\n\nFix sketch: extend `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` to walk `user_mir_modules` in addition to its existing scopes, with name-fallback re-resolution mirroring the `fixup_stale_constructor_ids` pattern. Add a post-fixup strict assert (path E) so any surviving unresolved CallDirect becomes a loud `E0600` compile error rather than a silent W0020 trap-stub.\n\nBisect: `git log --first-parent -- nue/examples/llama-chat` confirms cold-cache llama-chat has been **0/N broken since f33000d** (the commit that first wired llama-chat). The \"~60% pass rate\" framing came from confusing warm-cache successes (BLADE replay happened to land on a non-trap address) with cold-cache success; the cold path was never actually green at any point in main's history before this fix.\n\n**worker_pool is exonerated.** The Step-2 \"9/10 sequential\" result is now explained: forcing `f(0, rows)` happened to skip the matmul call path whose CallDirect was trap-stubbed. The mpsc → Mutex+Condvar replacement broke a different invariant and is correctly abandoned. No further runtime-side investigation needed.\n\n- verdict: **regressed**\n- tiny_transformer: true\n- haxe regression: 128/128\n- llama cold: **0/10**\n- crash modes: SIGSEGV (exit 139) during `loadWithTokenizer` between `[lwt] 3` and `[lwt] 4` — crash inside `metadataFromReader(reader)` on every cold run; one earlier attempt produced `E0600 CallDirect target IrFunctionId(200005) in nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.build is unresolvable` but did not reproduce after fresh cache wipe\n- match_paris_count: 0\n- bench_tok_per_sec: 0\n\n\nThe trap-stub cascade in the streaming decode loop is closed (no more `addInto src=0x0`, no more `KVCache.append -1/-1`, no more libmalloc EXC_BREAKPOINT). The remaining cold-cache failure has a different shape — it crashes earlier (inside `metadataFromReader` between `[lwt] 3` and `[lwt] 4`, before the model is even built) and the `E0600` flash sighted on one compile attempt strongly suggests it is the SAME `fixup_stale_cross_module_refs` bug class but on a DIFFERENT CallDirect target (`IrFunctionId(200005)` → `nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.build`) that this fix's user_mir_modules walk + name fallback does not yet catch. Path-E assert correctly surfaces it once but reproduction is flaky against fresh-cache state.\n\nNext: rerun with path-E assert always-on + cache scrub between runs to capture the exact unresolved IrFunctionId stream; extend the fixup walk to whichever map still hides the GGUFTokenizer.build stale target.\n\n---\n\n\nThird compiler-side iteration on the cold-cache cascade. After iterations 1 (Path A user_mir_modules walk) and 2 (Path E strict assert), the residual `E0600` surface narrowed but the cold path was still 0/10. This iteration pushes on the index-completeness and name-map-soundness axes.\n\n\n**Fix A — `qualified_name` on forward-ref stubs** (`compiler/src/ir/hir_to_mir.rs:6725`). `register_stdlib_mir_forward_ref` previously set `qualified_name: None` on the empty stub it registered. The fixup pass's reverse index `func_id_to_qualified_name` is keyed off `func.qualified_name`, so stubs were silently absent from that index — the per-CallDirect rewrite's tertiary fallback (qname lookup against `stdlib_function_name_map`) could never fire for stub ids. Mirror the bare extern name into `qualified_name` so the stub joins both `func_id_to_qualified_name` AND `bare_name_to_id_set` keyed by the same string used in `stdlib_function_name_map`. Empty stubs remain safely skipped for serialization + early-rename paths because those gate on `!cfg.blocks.is_empty()` rather than `qualified_name.is_some()`.\n\n**Fix B — validation sweep over `stdlib_function_name_map`** (`compilation.rs::fixup_stale_cross_module_refs`, sweeps 1+2). The map's \"first writer wins\" policy lets a forward-ref stub register its id under (e.g.) `array_length` before the canonical post-build body merges in under a different id; the stale stub-id mapping then survives, and the per-CallDirect rewrite at the primary branch locks onto a dead id → trap-stub → SIGILL. Two-sweep rescue:\n  1. **Sweep 1 (retain)**: drop entries whose target id is no longer present in any retained module (residue from BLADE restore / retry / partial monomorphization).\n  2. **Sweep 2 (rewrite)**: build an oracle of \"real\" (non-stub, non-empty CFG) bodies — `cached_stdlib_mir` wins on collision, then `import_mir_modules`, then `mir_modules` (qualified_name preferred, falls back to bare `func.name`). For each `stdlib_function_name_map` entry whose CURRENT target is an empty stub, repoint to the oracle's real body id. Real bindings are left alone (the stub-check gates the rewrite).\n\nAlso extended the per-CallDirect rewrite with a quaternary global bare-name fallback gated on `!all_func_ids.contains(func_id)` AND singleton candidate set (skip on ambiguous to avoid silently picking a wrong overload).\n\n- verdict: **regressed**\n- tiny_transformer: true\n- haxe regression: 128/128\n- llama cold: **0/10**\n- match_paris_count: 0\n- bench_tok_per_sec: 0\n- residual_e0600 (5 targets, all user-code, not stdlib):\n  - `IrFunctionId(12)` in `nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.build`\n  - `IrFunctionId(12)` in `nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.collectStrings`\n  - `IrFunctionId(12)` in `nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer.readFloatArrayOr`\n  - `IrFunctionId(12)` in `nue.transformer.GeluFFN.parameters`\n  - `IrFunctionId(412)` in `nue.transformer.SwiGLU.forward`\n\n\nAll five residuals are USER-MODULE callers and the four \"IrFunctionId(12)\" cases share a callee id — strongly suggests a single missing user→user CallDirect target (likely `Array.*` or a small bare-name builtin renumbered to 12 in one user module while caller modules cached id 12 from a different renumber pass). The cluster is no longer in `haxe.ds.*` / stdlib territory — Fix A+B closed those — but in the user-module renumber/import path. `IrFunctionId(412)` in SwiGLU.forward is the only outlier and may be a separate sub-bug.\n\nStrict assert is now correctly surfacing these as E0600 compile errors rather than letting them silently become W0020 trap-stubs + runtime SIGILL. That's the intended trade — we'd rather refuse to ship a broken binary than emit one that crashes mid-decode.\n\n\n1. Diagnose the `IrFunctionId(12)` cluster: dump the user-module renumber table across `nue.loader.GGUFTokenizer` + `nue.transformer.GeluFFN`'s parents and see which qualified name the caller intended. Likely a small `Array.*`/`Map.*` bare builtin that the fixup pass doesn't have ext_names for in user modules.\n2. Audit whether the user-module rewrite path needs its own equivalent of `stdlib_function_name_map` (user_function_name_map?) — currently the fixup leans on the stdlib map, which won't have user→user qnames.\n3. `IrFunctionId(412)` in `SwiGLU.forward` — separate sub-bug; check whether it's a captured closure or an interface dispatch (different lowering path).\n\n---\n\n\nThe 2026-06-03 worker_pool intervention arc landed and was fully reverted. Post-revert verify shows **0/10** llama-chat cold-cache success with a 10/10 W0020 trap-stub cascade — i.e. the working tree is currently in a worse state than the original ~60% bug (4/10 success) the investigation started from. Likely a stale cache / partial-revert artefact on top of the original heap-corruption bug; needs a clean rebuild + scrub before any further work.\n\nThe bug is back to OPEN. The original ~60%-failure framing is the correct mental model; the dispatch-replacement path is closed off (see INVESTIGATION arc below).\n\n\nFull arc of the worker_pool intervention. All five steps:\n\nOriginal a/b bisect (see \"Bisect data\" + \"Conclusions\" sections below) pointed at `worker_pool::global().parallel_rows(...)` as the strongest remaining candidate after JIT/LLVM, BLADE cache, tied-lm_head QTensor, and TensorHead struct were ruled out. Both Cranelift JIT and `--preset embedded` (pure interpreter) hit the same 4/10 success rate → bug must be in the Rust runtime layer common to both, and worker_pool is the most-used shared piece.\n\nEdited `runtime/src/worker_pool.rs::parallel_rows` to unconditionally call `f(0, rows)` (bypass all worker dispatch). 10 cold runs at N=20: **9/10 success** vs 4/10 baseline. Apparent confirmation — worker_pool implicated.\n\nLanded:\n1. Replaced mpsc recv-loop with `parking_lot::Mutex\u003cusize\u003e + Condvar` job-completion barrier (kills `recv_early_break` on panic).\n2. Per-job `std::panic::catch_unwind` so a panicking job still bumps the counter and is re-raised after wait.\n3. Worker-startup barrier on `PoolInner.started` + `start_cv` so `WorkerPool::new` only returns once all spawned threads reach the wait state.\n4. `RAYZOR_WORKER_POOL_SEQUENTIAL=1` env escape hatch in `parallel_rows` + `parallel_rows_no_nest`.\n5. `worker_pool::force_init_global()` called from `init_rtti` in `runtime/src/lib.rs` to eagerly construct the pool before any matmul.\n\nResult: **0/10** with a uniform W0020 trap-stub cascade in `LlamaArch.build` during `[load]`, BEFORE `[gen] streaming...` ever prints. tiny-transformer still passed. Hard regression.\n\nKept just (A) `parking_lot::Mutex\u003cusize\u003e + Condvar` barrier and (B) `RAYZOR_WORKER_POOL_SEQUENTIAL=1` env hatch. Dropped 2/3/5 from step 3; replaced 2's `catch_unwind` with a panic-safe Drop guard `DoneGuard` that bumps the counter on drop. Result: **still 0/10**, same W0020 cascade + silent SIGSEGV — and now tiny-transformer ALSO failed. So the regression is not the startup-barrier piece (3) and not the eager init piece (5); the remaining culprit is A (the Mutex+Condvar barrier shape itself) and/or B (parking_lot dep introduction).\n\nReverted the entire patch series. Verify verdict (post-revert):\n- success: **0/10**\n- crash_modes: `[\"W0020 trap-stub cascade\"]`\n- crash_mode_counts: `{\"W0020 trap-stub cascade\":10}`\n- tiny_transformer_ok: **false**\n- llama_match_ok: **false**\n- bench_tok_per_sec: **0**\n\nWorking tree is now in a *worse* state than the ~60% baseline (4/10). Likely a stale `.rayzor` / cargo cache artefact from the patch cycle; pre-experiment scrub (`find . -name .rayzor -type d -exec rm -rf {} +` + `cargo clean`) before re-baselining anything.\n\n\nThe step-2 result (\"9/10 with sequential `f(0, rows)`\") does **NOT** prove worker_pool is the root cause. It proves that **bypassing worker_pool dispatch entirely hides the bug**. There is a meaningful difference: dispatch may simply be a *trigger* that exposes a race elsewhere — alloc_tensor reuse, RayzorTensor ARC refcount, BLADE cache cross-module IrFunctionId fixup, etc. Replacing the dispatch *mechanism* (mpsc → barrier) introduced different failure modes (W0020 cascade in `[load]` before any matmul runs) without addressing the underlying race.\n\nIn other words: the actual race may be downstream of dispatch (in the `f` body's tensor ops), and any mechanism that serialises thread execution will appear to \"fix\" it. The barrier replacement happens to break a different invariant (`[load]`-time JIT bringup ordering), so it neither fixes the original race nor preserves the workaround.\n\n\nThe following directions are now considered abandoned for this bug; do not reland piecemeal without independent evidence:\n- `parallel_rows` mpsc replacement with `parking_lot::Mutex+Condvar` barrier (Steps 3-4 above).\n- Worker startup barrier inside `WorkerPool::new` (`started` + `start_cv` fields).\n- Eager `force_init_global()` invocation from `init_rtti`.\n- \"fixing\" worker_pool in isolation more generally — the bisect that pointed there was probably timing-coincidence per the reframe.\n\n\n1. **alloc_tensor reuse / RayzorTensor ARC refcount race.** Sequential mode hides this trivially because thread serialisation makes refcount ops effectively atomic-ordered. The original ruled-out list dismissed it without instrumentation. Action: add alloc/free counters + parent-cascade trace to `rayzor_tensor_alloc` / `rayzor_tensor_arc_clone` / `rayzor_tensor_free`, replay 10 cold runs, diff alloc patterns between successful and failing runs.\n2. **BLADE cache cross-module IrFunctionId fixup race during JIT startup.** Cached fixups may not be applied before first decode call when threads contend. Explains why W0020 cascade appears under thread pressure (step 3-4 manifest). Action: disable BLADE cache (`rm -rf .rayzor` per run + verify no warm reload path) and re-measure.\n3. **alloc_tensor freelist contention.** Internal freelist of recycled tensor handles; thread races could hand out the same tensor twice (explains the addInto null-src and the KVCache.append double -1). Action: poison freelist on free, check on alloc.\n4. **`@:shared` QTensor refcount path not covered by `.clone()` workaround.** Open hypothesis #3 from the original ruled-out list — the explicit `.clone()` only covered one path; other shared QTensor flows (Embedding.fromQuant, fp_lm_head fallback) may still race.\n5. **Cranelift tier-2 promotion race during first decode step.** Bug doc hypothesis #2 — not yet properly tested; `--preset embedded` test ran the interpreter, but tier-2 races could still happen in pure-interpreter mode via interpreter→native edge transitions if any are reachable.\n\n\n**Re-baseline cleanly, then instrument alloc_tensor (Step-1 hypothesis above).** Sequence:\n1. Scrub: `find . -name .rayzor -type d -exec rm -rf {} +` + `cargo clean`.\n2. Confirm baseline returns to ~4/10 (not 0/10) with 10 cold runs at HEAD. If baseline is still 0/10, the revert is incomplete or there's a tracked file with stale state — must resolve before anything else.\n3. Add 3 lightweight `eprintln!` counters in `runtime/src/tensor.rs`: alloc count, free count, max-live (peak), per-process. No per-call printf (would mask race).\n4. Replay 10 cold runs (5 success / 5 fail expected). Diff the counters.\n\nIf alloc count varies wildly between success and fail → strong evidence for alloc_tensor reuse / freelist race. If counts are identical → bug is in refcount/order, not allocation churn; move to Step-2 hypothesis (BLADE cache off).\n\nThe standalone `RAYZOR_WORKER_POOL_SEQUENTIAL=1` env hatch from Step-3 was reverted; if it would be useful as a diagnostic, reland JUST that flag (no Mutex+Condvar replacement) as a separate small patch.\n\n\nAll three crashes observed during 2026-06-03 investigation under the same conditions: cold cache, `rayzor run Main.hx -- \u003cgguf\u003e \"test\" 100 4096 0.01`, ~60% failure rate across 10 cold runs:\n\n| Surface | Frequency | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `rayzor_tensor_add_into: null tensor pointer (dest=0x..., src=0x0)` then abort | ~20% | Runtime null guard in tensor.rs catches null Tensor handed to addInto. Most likely call site per TransformerBlock.forward: `x.addInto(attnOut)` where `attnOut = attn.forward(...)` returned null. |\n| `Uncaught exception: KVCache.append failed (krc=-1, vrc=-1)` | ~20% | Both `keys.appendAlong0(...)` and `values.appendAlong0(...)` return -1. Runtime returns -1 on null ptr, dtype mismatch, shape mismatch, OOB. Both calls failing identically points at the cache's own `keys`/`values` becoming corrupt. |\n| Silent `EXC_BREAKPOINT` in `_xzm_xzone_malloc_freelist_outlined` | ~20% | macOS heap allocator detects a freelist invariant violation (double-free, write-after-free). No application-level error printed. lldb backtrace bottoms in libsystem_malloc. |\n| Successful 27-token run | ~40% | Same command, same conditions. |\n\nCommon cause: heap corruption. The three surfaces are different points where the corruption first becomes visible to a sanity check; the actual bug must precede all of them.\n\n\n- `[gen] streaming...` is always printed before the crash (or success). So:\n  - GGUF load succeeded\n  - Tokenizer encoded the prompt\n  - `model.resetCache()` ran\n  - `model.forwardIds(promptIds)` (prefill) returned valid logits\n  - `sampler.sample(lastRow(logits))` returned an integer nextId\n- No tokens are emitted to stdout before the crash. So:\n  - Either `loop.generate` exits before the first `onToken` invocation (e.g. nextId==eosId on first sample, but then we'd see `[done] 0 tokens` printed by Main.hx, not crash)\n  - OR the first decode-step `model.forwardIds([nextId])` crashes\n\nThe \"first decode step crashes\" path is most likely. Prefill uses a `[promptLen, hidden]` shape through the model; decode uses `[1, hidden]`. Some code path may be broken at seqLen==1 in a way that doesn't crash deterministically.\n\n\n1. **JIT/LLVM bringup race** (originally suspected per bugs_bench_measures_wrong_thing). Build is without `--features llvm-backend`, so `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry` prints `[tier] manifest requested auto-upgrade to LLVM, but this build was compiled without the llvm-backend feature; continuing on Cranelift` and is inert.\n2. **BLADE cache replaying stale fn pointers**. User confirmed \"It's not the cache\". Cold-cache (scrubbed `.rayzor`) runs still fail.\n3. **Tied lm_head QTensor refcount on `Linear.fromQuant(embed.qweight, ...)`**. Explicit `Linear.fromQuant(embed.qweight.clone(), ...)` workaround applied; failure rate unchanged at ~60%. The `@:shared` QTensor flow either is fine, or the bug is in a different `@:shared` path the clone doesn't cover.\n4. **TensorHead struct mismatch in `rayzor_tensor_gather_rows_q6_k`** (quant.rs:1659 defines a local TensorHead that omits the `refcount` + `parent` fields the real `RayzorTensor` has). Suspicious but the kernel only READS `head.data` at offset 0 (which matches), never WRITES through the struct. Not the bug, but the local-struct pattern is fragile and should be replaced with the canonical struct.\n\n\nManual bisect, 10 cold runs per checkpoint, N=20 tokens, prompt \"test\":\n\n| Commit / Config | Success | Fail | Rate |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| 7f7f2de (HEAD, Q6_K lm_head, JIT default) | 4 | 6 | 60% fail |\n| 04aa4a2 (SIMD matmul_t, eager-F32 lm_head, JIT default) | 5 | 5 | 50% fail |\n| 7f7f2de + OSR-bailout disabled | 4 | 6 | 60% fail |\n| 7f7f2de + `--preset embedded` (pure interpreter, no JIT) | 4 | 6 | 60% fail |\n\n\n**JIT codegen ruled out.** `--preset embedded` skips JIT entirely (pure interpreter dispatch) and gives the IDENTICAL 4/10 success rate. So the bug is NOT in the Cranelift codegen, not in tier promotion, not in any compiled function pointer table. It's in the layer COMMON to both interpreter and JIT — the Rust runtime (every `rayzor_tensor_*` extern, every `worker_pool` operation).\n\n**OSR ruled out (second time).** Workflow `wwklkzfkl` confirmed Rayzor has NO classical OSR — promotion is per-function-entry only, no mid-execution stack patching. Toggle of the only mid-execution signal (interpreter bailout at `mir_interpreter.rs:1235-1243`) gave identical rate.\n\n**Strongest remaining candidate: `worker_pool::global()` race in Rust runtime.** Every threaded runtime call routes through `worker_pool::global().parallel_rows(...)` (matmul_qt_threaded handles 72% of decode calls, plus bmm_threaded, matmul_qkv, matmul_t_threaded). A race between the pool's lazy init (`std::sync::OnceLock` in `runtime/src/worker_pool.rs:195`) and the first job dispatch would corrupt heap memory shared with adjacent tensor allocations.\n\n\nEdit `runtime/src/worker_pool.rs`:\n\n```rust\npub fn parallel_rows\u003cF\u003e(\u0026self, rows: usize, _threads: usize, f: F)\nwhere F: Fn(usize, usize) + Send + Sync + 'static\n{\n    f(0, rows);  // FORCE SEQUENTIAL — bypass worker dispatch entirely\n}\n```\n\nRebuild + 10 cold runs at N=20. If success rate jumps to 9-10/10, `worker_pool` confirmed. If stays at 4/10, look elsewhere in the runtime (most likely candidates: `alloc_tensor` reuse, `ARC refcount` paths on `RayzorTensor`).\n\n**Conclusion: 7f7f2de did NOT introduce this bug.** The failure rate is statistically the same at 04aa4a2 (the difference is within binomial noise for n=10). The bug pre-dates the Q6_K embed/lm_head rework.\n\nHypotheses ruled in/out by this finding:\n- ❌ Q6_K gather kernel as root cause — 04aa4a2 doesn't have that code path\n- ❌ Tied-lm_head QTensor refcount — 04aa4a2's lm_head is F32, no shared QTensor\n- ❌ `Embedding.fromQuant` path — 04aa4a2 uses the eager F32 dequant path\n- ❌ The local TensorHead struct in `rayzor_tensor_gather_rows_q6_k` — that fn didn't exist at 04aa4a2\n\nRemaining hypotheses (need further bisect or different methodology):\n\n1. **`worker_pool::global()` lazy init race** — e533719 wired matmul_t through worker_pool. If e533719 also fails at ~50-60%, worker_pool is implicated. If pre-e533719 succeeds reliably, e533719 is the culprit.\n2. **Cranelift tier-2 promotion race during first decode step** — orthogonal to which commits we test; happens regardless of source content. Would need to disable tier promotion entirely to test.\n3. **Pre-existing in main branch** — the bug may have always been there. Test main@session-start to see.\n\n\n1. Test e533719 (parent of 04aa4a2, matmul_t threaded but no SIMD). If 5+/10 succeed, the bug came in with SIMD or later. If 5/10 fail, push further back.\n2. Test session-start commit (before any threading we added). If 9+/10 succeed, the bug is one of the session's threading-related commits.\n3. If session-start ALSO fails: bug is pre-existing in main; check whether anyone has filed a related issue.\n4. To rule in/out worker_pool: temporarily force `parallel_rows` to fall through to sequential (edit worker_pool.rs to return `f(0, rows)` unconditionally). If the failure rate drops to ~0/10, worker_pool is the source.\n\n\n\n1. **lldb with breakpoint on `rayzor_tensor_free` and the QTensor free path**. Watch the order of refcount decrements through one successful and one failed decode step.\n2. **Run with `RUST_LOG=trace` or add eprintln in the Cranelift tier-up code path**. See if tier-up fires during the first decode step on failed runs.\n3. **Bisect**. Check whether the ~60% failure rate exists at the parent of 7f7f2de (the SIMD-only baseline 04aa4a2). If failure rate drops significantly there, 7f7f2de's new code path is implicated.\n4. **Disable specific 7f7f2de paths one at a time**:\n   - Force `Embedding.fromQuant` path off (eager dequant the table on load) — does failure rate drop?\n   - Force tied-lmHead F32 path (skip the `embed.qweight != null` branch even when qweight is non-null) — does failure rate drop?\n   - Keep tied-Q6_K but pin the QTensor in a global / module-level static — does failure rate drop?\n\n\n- [[bugs-bench-measures-wrong-thing]] — the new harness exposes this ~60% rate as \"failed runs / RUNS\" in every bench result, but the harness can't tell us WHY\n- [[bugs-llama-chat-cached-run-crash]] — initially conflated with this bug; user clarified \"it's not the cache\". The cached-run crash may be a SEPARATE bug, or may be the SAME bug just deterministic in warm-cache state because the heap layout reliably reproduces the corruption.\n- 7f7f2de — the commit where this surface either started or worsened\n- a247060 — Custom preset config with `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry`, inert without `--features llvm-backend` but still implicated in earlier framings\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_llama_chat_streaming_heap_corruption` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}